.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Vt OSSL_HTTP_REQ_CTX
is a context structure for an HTTP request and response, used to collect
all the necessary data to perform that request.
.Pp
This file documents low-level HTTP functions rarely used directly.
High-level HTTP client functions like
.Xr OSSL_HTTP_get 3
and
.Xr OSSL_HTTP_transfer 3
should be preferred.
.Pp
.Fn OSSL_HTTP_REQ_CTX_new
allocates a new HTTP request context structure, which gets populated
with the
.Vt BIO
to write/send the request to
.Pf ( Fa wbio Ns ),
the
.Vt BIO
to read/receive the response from
.Pf ( Fa rbio ,
which may be equal to
.Fa wbio Ns ),
and the maximum expected response header line length
.Fa buf_size .
A value <= 0 indicates that the
.Sy OSSL_HTTP_DEFAULT_MAX_LINE_LEN
of 4KiB should be used.
.Fa buf_size
is also used as the number of content bytes that are read at a time.
The allocated context structure includes an internal memory
.Vt BIO ,
which collects the HTTP request header lines.
.Pp
.Fn OSSL_HTTP_REQ_CTX_free
frees up the HTTP request context
.Fa rctx .
The
.Fa rbio
is not free'd,
.Fa wbio
will be free'd if
.Em free_wbio
is set.
If the argument is NULL, nothing is done.
.Pp
.Fn OSSL_HTTP_REQ_CTX_set_request_line
adds the 1st HTTP request line to
.Fa rctx .
The HTTP method is determined by
.Fa method_POST ,
which should be 1 to indicate
.Qo Li POST Qc
or 0 to indicate
.Qo Li GET Qc .
.Fa server
and
.Fa port
may be set to give the server and the optional port that an HTTP proxy
shall forward the request to, otherwise they must be left NULL.
.Fa path
provides the HTTP request path; if left NULL,
.Qo Li / Qc
is used.
For backward compatibility,
.Fa path
may begin with
.Qo Li http:// Qc
and thus convey an absoluteURI. In this case it indicates HTTP proxy use
and provides also the server (and optionally the port) that the proxy
shall forward the request to.
In this case the
.Fa server
and
.Fa port
arguments must be NULL.
.Pp
.Fn OSSL_HTTP_REQ_CTX_add1_header
adds header
.Fa name
with value
.Fa value
to the context
.Fa rctx .
It can be called more than once to add multiple header lines.
For example, to add a
.Qo Li Host Qc
header for
.Qo Li example.com Qc
you would call:
.Bd -literal
 OSSL_HTTP_REQ_CTX_add1_header(ctx, "Host", "example.com");
.Ed
.Pp
.Fn OSSL_HTTP_REQ_CTX_set_expected
optionally sets in
.Fa rctx
some expectations of the HTTP client on the response.
Due to the structure of an HTTP request, if the
.Fa keep_alive
argument is nonzero the function must be used before calling
.Fn OSSL_HTTP_REQ_CTX_set1_req .
.Pp
If the
.Fa expected_content_type
argument is not NULL, the client will check in a case-insensitive way
that the specified
.Qo Li Content-Type Qc
string value is included in the HTTP header of the response and return
an error if not.
In the
.Qo Li Content-Type Qc
header line the specified string should be present either as a whole, or
in case the specified string does not include a
.Qo Li ; Qc
character, it is sufficient that the specified string appears as a
prefix in the header line, followed by a
.Qo Li ; Qc
character and any further text.
For instance, if the
.Fa expected_content_type
argument specifies
.Qo Li text/html Qc ,
this is matched by
.Qo Li Text/HTML Qc ,
.Qo Li text/html; charset=UTF-8 Qc ,
etc.
.Pp
If the
.Fa expect_asn1
parameter is nonzero a structure in ASN.1 encoding will be expected as
the response content and input streaming is disabled.
This means that an ASN.1 sequence header is required, its length field
is checked, and
.Fn OSSL_HTTP_REQ_CTX_get0_mem_bio
should be used to get the buffered response.
Otherwise (by default) any input format is allowed, with body length
checks being performed on error messages only.
In this case the BIO given as
.Fa rbio
argument to
.Fn OSSL_HTTP_REQ_CTX_new
should be used directly to read the response contents, which may support
streaming.
.Pp
If the
.Fa timeout
parameter is > 0 this indicates the maximum number of seconds the
subsequent HTTP transfer (sending the request and receiving a response)
is allowed to take.
.Fa timeout
== 0 enables waiting indefinitely, i.e., no timeout can occur.
This is the default.
.Fa timeout
< 0 takes over any value set via the
.Em overall_timeout
argument of
.Xr OSSL_HTTP_open 3
with the default being 0, which means no timeout.
.Pp
If the
.Fa keep_alive
parameter is 0, which is the default, the connection is not kept open
after receiving a response.
This is the default behavior for HTTP 1.0. If the value is 1 or 2 then a
persistent connection is requested.
If the value is 2 then a persistent connection is required, i.e., an
error occurs in case the server does not grant it.
.Pp
.Fn OSSL_HTTP_REQ_CTX_set1_req
finalizes the HTTP request context.
It is needed if the
.Fa method_POST
parameter in the
.Fn OSSL_HTTP_REQ_CTX_set_request_line
call was 1 and an ASN.1-encoded request should be sent.
It must also be used when requesting "keep-alive", even if a GET request
is going to be sent, in which case
.Fa req
must be NULL. Unless
.Fa req
is NULL, the function adds the DER encoding of
.Fa req
using the ASN.1 template
.Fa it
to do the encoding (which does not support streaming).
The HTTP header
.Qo Li Content-Length Qc
is filled out with the length of the request.
.Fa content_type
must be NULL if
.Fa req
is NULL. If
.Fa content_type
isn't NULL, the HTTP header
.Qo Li Content-Type Qc
is also added with the given string value.
The header lines are added to the internal memory
.Vt BIO
for the request header.
.Pp
.Fn OSSL_HTTP_REQ_CTX_nbio
attempts to send the request prepared in
.Fa rctx
and to gather the response via HTTP, using the
.Fa wbio
and
.Fa rbio
that were given when calling
.Fn OSSL_HTTP_REQ_CTX_new .
The function may need to be called again if its result is -1, which
indicates
.Xr BIO_should_retry 3 .
In such a case it is advisable to sleep a little in between, using
.Xr BIO_wait 3
on the read BIO to prevent a busy loop.
See
.Fn OSSL_HTTP_REQ_CTX_set_expected
how the response content type, the response body, the HTTP transfer
timeout, and "keep-alive" are treated.
Any error message body is consumed if a
.Qo Li Content-Type Qc
header is not included or its value starts with
.Qo Li text/ Qc .
This is used for tracing the body contents if HTTP tracing is enabled.
If the
.Qo Li Content-Length Qc
header is present in the response and its value exceeds the maximum
allowed response content length or the response is an error message with
its body length exceeding this value or the content is an ASN.1-encoded
structure with a length exceeding this value or both length indications
are present but disagree then an error occurs.
.Pp
.Fn OSSL_HTTP_REQ_CTX_nbio_d2i
is like
.Fn OSSL_HTTP_REQ_CTX_nbio
but on success in addition parses the response, which must be a
DER-encoded ASN.1 structure, using the ASN.1 template
.Fa it
and places the result in
.Em *pval .
.Pp
.Fn OSSL_HTTP_REQ_CTX_exchange
calls
.Fn OSSL_HTTP_REQ_CTX_nbio
as often as needed in order to exchange a request and response or until
a timeout is reached.
On success it returns a pointer to the BIO that can be used to read the
result.
If an ASN.1-encoded response was expected, this is the BIO returned by
.Fn OSSL_HTTP_REQ_CTX_get0_mem_bio
when called after the exchange.
This memory BIO does not support streaming.
Otherwise the returned BIO is the
.Fa rbio
given to
.Fn OSSL_HTTP_REQ_CTX_new ,
which may support streaming.
When this BIO is returned, it has been read past the end of the response
header, such that the actual response body can be read from it.
The returned BIO pointer MUST NOT be freed by the caller.
.Pp
.Fn OSSL_HTTP_REQ_CTX_get0_mem_bio
returns the internal memory
.Vt BIO .
Before the HTTP request is sent, this could be used to adapt its header
lines.
.Em Use with caution!
After receiving a response via HTTP, the BIO represents the current
state of reading the response header.
If the response was expected to be ASN.1 encoded, its contents can be
read via this BIO, which does not support streaming.
The returned BIO pointer must not be freed by the caller.
.Pp
.Fn OSSL_HTTP_REQ_CTX_get_resp_len
returns the size of the response contents in
.Fa rctx
if provided by the server as
.Qo Li Content-Length Qc
header field, else 0.
.Pp
.Fn OSSL_HTTP_REQ_CTX_set_max_response_length
sets the maximum allowed response content length for
.Fa rctx
to
.Fa len .
If not set or
.Fa len
is 0 then the
.Sy OSSL_HTTP_DEFAULT_MAX_RESP_LEN
is used, which currently is 100 KiB.
.Pp
.Fn OSSL_HTTP_REQ_CTX_set_max_response_hdr_lines
changes the limit for the number of HTTP header lines allowed to be
received in a response.
The default limit is
.Sy OSSL_HTTP_DEFAULT_MAX_RESP_HDR_LINES ,
currently 256. If the limit is not 0 and the number of lines exceeds the
limit, then the HTTP_R_RESPONSE_TOO_MANY_HDRLINES error is indicated.
Setting the limit to 0 disables the check.
.Pp
.Fn OSSL_HTTP_is_alive
can be used to query if the HTTP connection given by
.Fa rctx
is still alive, i.e., has not been closed.
It returns 0 if
.Fa rctx
is NULL.
.Pp
If the client application requested or required a persistent connection
and this was granted by the server, it can keep
.Fa rctx
as long as it wants to send further requests and
.Fn OSSL_HTTP_is_alive
returns nonzero, else it should call
.Em OSSL_HTTP_REQ_CTX_free(rctx)
or
.Xr OSSL_HTTP_close 3 .
In case the client application keeps
.Fa rctx
but the connection then dies for any reason at the server side, it will
notice this obtaining an I/O error when trying to send the next request
via
.Fa rctx .
.Sh WARNINGS
The server's response may be unexpected if the hostname that was used to
create the
.Fa wbio ,
any
.Qo Li Host Qc
header, and the host specified in the request URL do not match.
.Pp
Many of these functions must be called in a certain order.
.Pp
First, the HTTP request context must be allocated:
.Fn OSSL_HTTP_REQ_CTX_new .
.Pp
Then, the HTTP request must be prepared with request data:
.Bl -enum
.It
Calling
.Fn OSSL_HTTP_REQ_CTX_set_request_line .
.It
Adding extra header lines with
.Fn OSSL_HTTP_REQ_CTX_add1_header .
This is optional and may be done multiple times with different names.
.It
Finalize the request using
.Fn OSSL_HTTP_REQ_CTX_set1_req .
This may be omitted if the GET method is used and "keep-alive" is not
requested.
.El
.Pp
When the request context is fully prepared, the HTTP exchange may be
performed with
.Fn OSSL_HTTP_REQ_CTX_nbio
or
.Fn OSSL_HTTP_REQ_CTX_exchange .
.Sh NOTES
When built with tracing enabled,
.Fn OSSL_HTTP_REQ_CTX_nbio
and all functions using it, such as
.Fn OSSL_HTTP_REQ_CTX_exchange
and
.Xr OSSL_HTTP_transfer 3 ,
may be traced using
.Sy OSSL_TRACE_CATEGORY_HTTP .
See also
.Xr OSSL_trace_enabled 3
and
.Xr openssl-env 7 .
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn OSSL_HTTP_REQ_CTX_new
returns a pointer to a
.Vt OSSL_HTTP_REQ_CTX ,
or NULL on error.
.Pp
.Fn OSSL_HTTP_REQ_CTX_free ,
.Fn OSSL_HTTP_REQ_CTX_set_max_response_length ,
and
.Fn OSSL_HTTP_REQ_CTX_set_max_response_hdr_lines
do not return values.
.Pp
.Fn OSSL_HTTP_REQ_CTX_set_request_line ,
.Fn OSSL_HTTP_REQ_CTX_add1_header ,
.Fn OSSL_HTTP_REQ_CTX_set1_req ,
and
.Fn OSSL_HTTP_REQ_CTX_set_expected
return 1 for success and 0 for failure.
.Pp
.Fn OSSL_HTTP_REQ_CTX_nbio
and
.Fn OSSL_HTTP_REQ_CTX_nbio_d2i
return 1 for success, 0 on error or redirection, -1 if retry is needed.
.Pp
.Fn OSSL_HTTP_REQ_CTX_exchange
and
.Fn OSSL_HTTP_REQ_CTX_get0_mem_bio
return a pointer to a
.Vt BIO
on success as described above or NULL on failure.
The returned BIO must not be freed by the caller.
.Pp
.Fn OSSL_HTTP_REQ_CTX_get_resp_len
returns the size of the response contents or 0 if not available or an
error occurred.
.Pp
.Fn OSSL_HTTP_is_alive
returns 1 if its argument is non-NULL and the client requested a
persistent connection and the server did not disagree on keeping the
connection open, else 0.
